\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee seen weakening gradually vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Giri Venkatesan \par \uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, April 16 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to weaken gradually to around 40.25 per dollar by the end of June and further to 41.50-42.0 by March 1999, dealers said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "We are beginning to see a bit of corporate (dollar) demand hit the market," said the chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a state-run bank. "Earlier, corporates were under the impression the new government would be in favour of a strong rupee." \par \par \uc2  The coalition led by the Hindu nationalist Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) took power last month amid widespread expectations it would usher in policies leading to a strong rupee to boost exports and protect Indian industry. \par \par \uc2  But on Monday, Commerce Minister Ramakrishna Hedge said he did not think a fall in the value of the rupee affected exports. \par \par \uc2  "Exchange rate management will have to balance the needs of the exporters as well as the other macroeconomic requirements for maintaining price stability," Hedge said. \par \par \uc2  "I think the message is quite clear," the state-run bank dealer said. "The government does not mind a slow depreciation in the rupee." \par \par \uc2  Five bankers polled by Reuters said they saw the rupee touching 40.25 per dollar by the end of June. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could weaken further to 41.50-42.0 by March 1999, they said. \par \par \uc2  A former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official - who also headed a committee to guide the country towards capital account convertibility - on Wednesday said the rupee was overvalued by around 6-7 percent in real effective exchange rate terms. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the immediate trigger behind a near 25 paise (0.25 rupee) fall in the rupee's value this week could have been technical - triggered by pent-up dollar demand after banks were shut for holidays last week. \par \par \uc2  "The holidays did lead to a bunching of corporate demand," said a dealer at an American bank. "But the point is that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) is not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively in the market and it appears they are happy with a slow slide." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mopped up dollars from banks. \par \par \uc2  "When these banks bought in the market to cover their positions after selling to the RBI, it started the rupee's fall on Monday," the American bank dealer said. "But then corporate demand started to trickle in, weakening the rupee further." \par \par \uc2  The rupee was quoted around 39.71/72 per dollar in afternoon trade on Thursday. It opened on Monday at 39.51/54. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said there had not yet been a rush of corporate demand that could push the rupee into a free fall. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment much of the demand is from banks or corporates who have taken loans against foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Exporters are holding back a bit, waiting to see if the rupee will dip further and give them better realisations. Further premiums are low and exporters do not see much profit in selling at these levels," he added. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4g07cm8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
