\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Open -2: No Big Dollar Demand Seen - Dealers \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 05:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee (INR) opened little changed against the dollar Wednesday in a quiet interbank market because dealers said that there isn't expected to be any major merchant demand for dollars 'unusually' at the end of the financial year, March 31, 1998, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened little changed Tuesday at INR39.52-.53, from its Tuesday's close of INR39.520-.525. \par \par \uc2  At 0420 GMT (11:20 p.m. EST), the dollar is trading slightly higher at 39.530-.535 on some small interbank buying. \par \par \uc2  'It is a small movement and no reason can be given to it,' said a dealer at a U.S. bank. \par \par \uc2  They also said that the dollar will be in a tight range because of dealers positive sentiment on the coalition government led by Bharatiya Janata Party. \par \par \uc2  'It is very unusual that there isn't much of a merchant demand for dollars at the end of the financial year,' said a senior dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  He explained that usually at the end of the year there is dollar buying for payment of their imports. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that in the last couple of months when the rupee was sliding against the dollar importers had panicked and bought a lot of forward dollars. \par \par \uc2  'Probably most of these importers had bought excess dollars and would be selling it back' keeping the market with more than enough dollar supplies, said the dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a foreign bank said that the dollar is expected to be in a tight range of INR39.50-.55 because of State Bank of India, the country's largest commercial bank, and the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  He said if banks go and offer dollars to the RBI both in the spot and the forward market 'than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accepts these trades and buys dollars.' \par \par \uc2  He said that the RBI is also swapping its March-end forward contracts for April-end delivery. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that the rupee premiums for the six-month forward dollar will range between 7.00% and 7.50%, below the 8.00% rate fixed by the RBI on its daily repurchase agreements of government securities. \par \par \uc2  -By Raghavendra Upadhyaya; (91 22) 288-4211 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 25-03-98 \par \par \uc2  0433GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du3p02j7j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
