\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends weaker on strong dlr demand. \b0\par\par\uc2 472 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Feb 23 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee hit a month low against the dollar on strong demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from banks and corporates, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed at 39.25/28 per dollar against its previous close of 38.92/95, they said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a sudden surge in dollar demand weakened the rupee past the 39 per dollar level. \par \par \uc2  Sustained buying interest during the day and a lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) pushed the rupee down to 39.20/23 by 4:00 p.m. (1030 GMT), they said. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee fell through 39 within about 45 minutes of start of trade," said a dealer at a foreign bank. "It was under pressure right through the day." \par \par \uc2  Late evening covering of short positions by a few foreign banks weakened it further to a close of 39.25/28. \par \par \uc2  "There is strong underlying demand and we expect the market to open around this level tomorrow," said a dealer at a private bank. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was dollar demand on Monday from both banks and corporates. \par \par \uc2  "Initially there was demand only from a few foreign banks who were probably covering shorts after they were hit on Friday," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  "But later in the morning other banks joined in the dollar-buying spree," he said. "And as the rupee kept weakening with no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , corporates also came in." \par \par \uc2  Dealers were mixed on the factors driving the surge in demand on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "It could have been banks' covering of short positions or their buying in anticipation of corporate month-end demand," said a private bank dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Further, the year end is also not very far off and there is bound to be increased demand on this account as well," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Indian fiscal year ends in March. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers said uncertainty over the results of ongoing elections could also have induced some corporates to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  Monday was the third day of voting in elections to elect India's parliament -- the second election in as many years. \par \par \uc2  Opinion polls point to a hung parliament. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupee rebounded from morning lows against the forward dollar to end Monday slightly firmer than the weekend close. \par \par \uc2  Premiums on six month dollars closed at 15.79 percent after hitting 16.52 percent in afternoon trade. They closed the weekend at 15.99 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Premiums were up initially on demand as importers came to the market, reacting to the rupee's fall in spot," said a dealer. "But they eased later after some state-run banks entered the market as receivers (of premiums)." ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2n01cc3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
