\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-India rupee stability hinges on majority govt. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Beverly Mathews \par \uc2 892 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Februar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Feb 22 (Reuters) - A majority government would offer a stable plank for India's economic policy-making and ensure steady growth, shielding the rupee from sharp fluctuations, bankers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The economic reforms process is irreversible and whichever party comes into power, economic policy won't change much," said Ravi Pai, vice president, treasury and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the private sector HDFC Bank. \par \par \uc2  He said a majority government would encourage foreign institutional investors who prefer India's steady reform process to the confusion now reigning in Southeast Asian countries. \par \par \uc2  But the rupee was likely to depreciate irrespective of which party came to power, Pai said. \par \par \uc2  "Immaterial of which government comes into power, the authorities want the rupee to depreciate by a certain percentage on a crawling basis," he said. "They will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support this objective." \par \par \uc2  The second leg of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's general elections began on Sunday. The first phase last Monday was marred by violence and charges of vote rigging. Opinion polls have suggested no single party will get the majority needed to form a government. \par \par \uc2  The elections, the second in two years, were forced on the country when the Congress party last year withdrew its support to the minority 15-party United Front coalition government. \par \par \uc2  The fragility of India's coalition politics was again exposed on Saturday when the government of Uttar Pradesh, the country's most populous state, was dismissed by the state governor after it was deserted by a coalition ally. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a stable government would boost the economic reforms process started in the early 1990s and ensure steady growth. India's gross domestic product (GDP) has grown by an annual average of around 7.0 percent over the last three years. \par \par \uc2  A strong elected government combined with existing fundamentals could help \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability and keep prices down, Citicorp International Securities Limited said in a research report this month. \par \par \uc2  "A stable rupee, reduced oil prices, large food reserves and a manageable deficit will keep inflation within four to five percent," Citicorp's report said. \par \par \uc2  Despite falling by some 8.3 percent in the last six months the rupee has been stable since the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) hiked interest rates in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  defence package after it hit a historic low of 40.45 per dollar on January 15. \par \par \uc2  It closed on Friday at 38.92/95. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's fall came amid a slowdown in capital inflows and increased competition from plummeting Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  If the elections resulted in a majority government or a majority-led coalition the rupee was unlikely to depreciate more than the rate warranted by inflation, Citicorp said. \par \par \uc2  A coalition without ideological agreement would slow the pace of reforms and cause stagnant development, the report said. \par \par \uc2  This would hurt industry and pile pressure on the rupee, it said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee might depreciate 8-10 percent a year in such a case, the report warned. \par \par \uc2  India's year-on-year wholesale price indexed inflation rose to 5.42 percent in the week ended February 7 from 5.29 percent the previous week and compared to 7.83 percent a year ago. \par \par \uc2  "The key question is whether the portfolio inflows, which showed signs of picking up in February after declining during November to January, will be sustained," said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultant A V Rajwade. \par \par \uc2  Net investments by foreign institutional investors rose $36.1 million during February 1-13, reversing a three-month trend, India's capital market regulator said. \par \par \uc2  Net FII outflows in November-January were $385.8 million. \par \par \uc2  Rajwade said India could face competition in attracting foreign investments as other Asian economies recovered. \par \par \uc2  "If portfolio investments (into India) are positive then the rupee will be fairly stable over the next seven months," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Since industrial activity has picked up only moderately, import demand is not much," he added. \par \par \uc2  Industrial production grew by 4.7 percent in April-December 1997 compared to the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  Bankers said the level  of interest rates was a key factor determining the stability of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee stabilised after mid-January, thanks largely to sledgehammer tactics used by the RBI," said the country treasurer of a foreign bank, who did not want to be named. \par \par \uc2  The RBI hiked banks' cash reserve ratio requirements by 50 basis points to 10.5 percent. It also raised the rate at which it lends to commercial banks to 11 percent from nine. \par \par \uc2  "After elections, there will definitely be pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates to help industrial recovery," he said. \par \par \uc2  "Lower interest rates could fuel speculation on the rupee, given that other factors such as political instability will undermine sentiment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the banker said. \par \par \uc2  The treasurer said the RBI resorted to hiking interest rates after foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves had been drawn down, in part due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the rupee in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the last quarter of 1997 and January 1998. \par \par \uc2  India's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $24.11 billion on February 13, the RBI said on Saturday. This represents a $1.74 billion increase in the current financial year (April-March) but is considerably lower than the $26.43 billion it reported at the end of August 1997. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2m01e9t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
