\par\par \b \uc2  India Fin Sec -3: RBI Reserves Wisely Invested Abroad \b0\par\par\uc2 180 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 1998\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Ahluwalia said that though the current consensus is that an excessive flow of funds led to the southeast Asian crisis, the fault seems to lie with the international finance companies who invested too much in certain sectors. \par \par \uc2  The other fault with some of the Southeast Asian countries is that they invested more than half of their reserves with banks and finance companies \par \par \uc2  In contrast, the Reserve Bank of India has put all its reserves in high quality investments abroad. He pointed out that the RBI's forward \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is limited to 10% of its total reserves. \par \par \uc2  The RBI, \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market inthe past few weeks though sell-buy swaps to prop the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Ahluwalia added that there's no prospect, so far as reserves go, for India to fall into a trap as in Korea and Thailand but cautioned that 'we don't relapse into a gleeful complacency.' \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-02-98 \par \par \uc2  0630GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du2j01ipw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
