\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Up In Midday Trade; No Corporate Interest Noted \b0\par\par\uc2 245 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 1998\par 08:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is up slightly against the dollar in midday trading Friday with the market convinced the tight grip of the RBI on spot dollars will be maintained. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said corporate action was nearly non-existent. \par \par \uc2  At 0740 GMT (2:40 a.m. EST), the dollar is trading between 38.72 rupees (INR) and INR38.74, up marginally from between INR38.74-.77 at the close of trading Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Friday between INR38.75-77, and has remained a narrow 0.07 rupee-range until midday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that a brief \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trade when it swapped spot dollars for February dollars prevented banks from trying to make the spot dollar stronger or the forwards weaker. \par \par \uc2  In the past three days the RBI has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through sell-buy swaps to keep the rupee from sliding and it is expected to keep its vigil until a new government assumes office after the general elections starting mid-February. \par \par \uc2  The RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past week indicate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would like to see the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  between INR38.50 and INR39.00 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  For the latest indicative rupee rates, please see Dow Jones page [26501]. \par \par \uc2  -By Latha Venkatesh; 91-22-2884211; lvenkatesh@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) D