\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee sinks below key 40 per dollar barrier. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 1998\par 13:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Jan 14 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee broke through the key psychological barrier of 40 to the dollar early Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  watched and the government played it down. \par \par \uc2  India's finance secretary Montek Singh Ahluwalia said the government was "not alarmed". \par \par \uc2  "The RBI (Reserve Bank of India) is of course watching the market carefully and we are not alarmed at the developments," Ahluwalia told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed at 40.13/15 to the dollar after hitting a historic low of 40.22. \par \par \uc2  It started at 39.89/94 against the previous close of 39.88/90 and slipped past 40 within minutes of opening. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has weakened over 12 percent since August 1997, in contrast to most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have fallen much further. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has hardened against all \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past few days and focusing on the rupee-dollar rate gives an exaggerated picture of the situation," Ahluwalia said. \par \par \uc2  He said the government had made it clear it does not propose to defend the rupee at any particular level. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee has become an orphan," said the chief dealer at a public sector bank, referring to absence of Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the rupee pierced the 40 per dollar level. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's decline has been slowed to a crawl by a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to kill corporate and interbank speculation since December. \par \par \uc2  This meant that all dollars have to be backed by genuine trade transactions, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But a worsening dollar demand-supply mismatch ensured that the rupee weakened and the RBI has been shy in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market for fear of depletion of reserves, they said. \par \par \uc2  RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been mostly in the swap market which is reserve-neutral, the dealers said. \par \par \uc2  RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market only after the rupee sank to 40.20 level on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the rupee's further fall would be easier now that the 40-per dollar hump has been crossed. \par \par \uc2  "Forty has now become the base," Ravi Kumar, chief dealer at ABN-AMRO Bank said. "We are looking at a 40-42 (rupees to the dollar) band for the first quarter of 1998." \par \par \uc2  He said Indian importers were keenly watching the developments in South-east Asia. If the major Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilise, it would have a positive impact on the Indian rupee, he said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee could stabilise at 40-41 to the dollar if a stable government is installed after the general elections in February-March, Kumar said. \par \par \uc2  Leading Indian firms said the rupee's weakening proved that India was no longer insulated from Asian developments. \par \par \uc2  S.D. Kulkarni, managing director of engineering firm Larsen & Toubro Ltd said India was no longer insulated from developments in rest of Asia. \par \par \uc2  "The depreciation of South-east Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to have some impact on us," he said. Other industrialists agreed. \par \par \uc2  "It (the rupee) will stabilise (at current levels)," Keshub Mahindra, chairman of automobile firm Mahindra & Mahindra said. \par \par \uc2  "But we have to see how the East-Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go." \par \par \uc2  The rupee was buoyant through most of 1996 and 1997 on strong capital flows, forcing the RBI to buy dollars as recently as October 1997, to prevent the rupee from rising. \par \par \uc2  But foreign portfolio investments into India suffered as investors pulled out funds in the wake of the Asian stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1e06kop\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
