\par\par \b \uc2  Indian firms see rupee stabilise at new level. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Anantharaman Muralikumar \par \uc2 371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Jan 14 (Reuters) - Leading Indian industrialists said on Wednesday the rupee should stabilise at the new, weaker level after it breached the crucial 40 per dollar barrier in early morning trade. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened Wednesday at 39.89/94 to the dollar compared with the previous close of 39.88/90 and slipped past the psychological barrier of 40 in early morning trade. \par \par \uc2  It has fallen over 12 percent since August 1997. \par \par \uc2  Bank dealers had forecast on Tuesday the rupee would test the 40 barrier on Wednesday unless the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) stepped in only after the 40 level was breached. \par \par \uc2  Keshub Mahindra, chairman of automobile firm Mahindra & Mahindra Ltd said he expected the rupee to stabilise at the new level. \par \par \uc2  "It (rupee level) should stabilise. But we have to see how the East Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go," Mahindra told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  S.D. Kulkarni, managing director and chief executive officer of engineering conglomerate Larsen & Toubro Ltd said India was no longer insulated from developments in the neighbouring countries. \par \par \uc2  "Current levels (of rupee) should be okay," Kulkarni said. \par \par \uc2  "We are not insulated today. The depreciation of the South-east Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to have some impact on us." \par \par \uc2  "There has to be some sort of competitive devaluation." \par \par \uc2  Hari L. Mundra, chief financial officer at diversified business group RPG Enterprises, said Wednesday's move was the result of recent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures and a thin interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "This is a result of panic in a very thin market because of RBI's measures," he said, referring to RBI's order earlier this month asking banks to square up their end of day dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "At this juncture, the fall is not because of fundamental issues. The RBI needs to take a relook at its measures," he said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee was trading at 40.12/17 at 12.35 p.m. \par \par \uc2  India's finance secretary Montek Singh Ahluwalia told Reuters in New Delhi there was no cause for alarm. ((Bombay newsroom, +91 22 265-9000 fax +91 22 264-1699, Bombay.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1e06kmr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
