\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee Opens Unchanged, Slips On Early Dlr Demand \b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 1998\par 05:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee opened unchanged against the dollar Tuesday but slipped immediately and is trading very close to its all-time low, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Tuesday at 39.80-.85 rupees (INR). It had closed Monday at INR39.82-.84. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is trading at INR39.85-.87 at 0345 GMT (10:45 p.m. EST). Traders said it has been traded at INR39.87 Tuesday. The dollar traded at a record high of INR39.95 on Dec. 2. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said while there is small early demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there isn't much supply in the market. \par \par \uc2  'State (Bank) hasn't sold anything in the market but his price indicates that he is a seller,' a dealer at a private sector bank said. \par \par \uc2  As reported, continuous dollar sales by the State Bank prevented the rupee from falling Friday and Monday. \par \par \uc2  State Bank is the country's largest commercial bank and its actions often set the trend for the market because it deals on behalf of several large corporations, including state-owned oil companies. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar is likely to rise above INR39.90 Tuesday unless the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell spot dollars. \par \par \uc2  But others said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may stay out of the market and let the State Bank do its bidding instead. The State Bank's dollar sales in recent days are perceived to be prompted by the RBI. \par \par \uc2  Trading volumes are low because the RBI Wednesday directed banks to hold square or near-square positions at the end of each trading day until further notice. The measure was aimed at easing the downward pressure on the rupee, which the RBI believes was mainly caused by interbank speculation. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the forward premiums are likely to fall slightly after the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sells forward-dollars after buying spot dollars. The RBI had been swapping forward dollars almost every session over the last three weeks to prevent the forward premiums from rising. \par \par \uc2  The premium for benchmark July dollars closed Monday between INR2.10 and INR2.15, down from INR2.25-.40 at the close of trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  For the latest indicative rupee rates, please see Dow Jones page [26501]. \par \par \uc2  -By Pankaj Aher; (91-22) 288-4211; paher@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 13-01-98 \par \par \uc2  0416GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du1d00aav\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
