\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee slides through 39 per dlr, RBI absent. \b0\par\par\uc2 294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Dec 1 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee continued to seek fresh lows in Monday afternoon interbank trade, hitting 39 per dollar against its previous close of 38.55/58, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Deals have been done at the 39.04 level," said a dealer at a Bombay bank. "The market has been one-way right from the open." \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened Monday at 38.60/70 per dollar against its weekend close of 38.55/58. \par \par \uc2  At 1517 p.m. (0947 GMT), the rupee was quoted 39.04/07 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Reserve of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market until late afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "It is fairly puzzling but I think the fact that there has not been very sharp movements might have influenced them to stay away," said a dealer. "The rupee has certainly dropped but the fall has been fairly orderly." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the RBI checked for prices but did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit its earlier record low of 38.85 on Friday before rallying on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  Later on Friday, RBI announced a package of measures to speed up inward remittances of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by exporters and to mop up excess domestic liquidity to prevent banks using the extra funds to speculate in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said forward premiums had risen on corporate demand driven by the rupee's weakening in spot deals and by the prospect of a tightening in domestic liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The premium for six-month dollars was quoted at 7.48 percent in afternoon trade against the weekend close of 6.51 percent. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc101rdc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
