\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee's floor seen set by RBI  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 28 (Reuters) - The rupee's fall was checked at 38.60 to the dollar level on Friday by repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) came in three rounds, selling dollars after the rupee breached the 38.60-level, they said. \par \par \uc2  "I see a 38.30/60 range for some time," said Ravi Pai, chief dealer at HDFC Bank in Bombay. "People are a little nervous but there is no panic buying (of dollars)," he said. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Indian commerce minister Bolla Buli Ramaiah said the "rupee would take its own course." \par \par \uc2  "Right now what is happening is due to political reasons," Ramaiah told Reuters in New Delhi. \par \par \uc2  "People are theoretically connecting politics with the rupee," said a dealer, referring to political uncertainties affecting the survival of the ruling United Front government. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the impact of politics on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was grossly exaggerated. \par \par \uc2  "Personally I don't consider them (politics) as relevant," HDFC Bank's Pai said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit an alltime low of 38.85 in early morning trade, triggered by a newspaper report which suggested the government was thinking of a five percent trading band around 38.40 within which the rupee should trade. \par \par \uc2  An RBI official later denied the report. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has fallen by about 8.0 percent against the dollar since August. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1