\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee should fall further - economists. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Cameron-Moore \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 1997\par 14:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 23 (Reuters) - Already six percent down in the last three months and saddled with a government in danger of falling any day, the rupee needs to depreciate further to fit into the changed Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape, economists said on Sunday. \par \par \uc2  Ashok Gulati, chief economist at the National Council for Applied Economic Research in New Delhi said officials at both the World Bank and International Monetary Fund privately advocated a substantially weaker rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The feeling in Washington is that the rupee has to go down more," Gulati said. \par \par \uc2  IMF and World Bank economists believed a depreciation against the dollar by a further seven to eight percent was warranted, he said, adding that the greater part of the move could happen as early as the next 15 days to one month. \par \par \uc2  "Political instability is a contributory factor, but it is the economics around the world that are driving the depreciation in the rupee," Gulati said. \par \par \uc2  S.P Gupta, director of the Indian Council for Research on International Economic Relations, said the rupee should be nearer 40 to the dollar and "the sooner the better." \par \par \uc2  The rupee fell to a 21 month low of 37.75/80 to the dollar on Friday, dropping 30 paise from the previous close. \par \par \uc2  But it is still high, if not spry, compared with the devastated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Asia's wounded Tiger economies, which have suffered falls anywhere between 10 and 40 percent in the last 10 months. \par \par \uc2  Gulati and Gupta are far from being isolated voices, but others are more wary about sticking their necks out publicly. \par \par \uc2  A number of economists at foreign brokerages here see the rupee falling but because of sensitivities over being guests in the country are wary of saying so openly just yet. \par \par \uc2  Two privately forecast a rupee at 40 per dollar by the end of March, while a third said he would like it to fall beyond that in the next eight to 10 months. \par \par \uc2  Others like the Peregrine India brokerage saw it going to 38.5 and recovering to 37.40 by March. While Sandeep Nanda at NatWest Markets said he was sticking by a forecast of 38.30 by March, but added it was under review. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb any exchange rate volatility, and sold more than $1 billion in just over a week doing just that. \par \par \uc2  The RBI has heeded IMF advice that the market should be allowed to determine the rupee's level, but it seems the market is moving too fast for comfort. \par \par \uc2  Chakravarty Rangarajan, who handed over governorship of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Bimal Jalan on Saturday, told the market last Tuesday it had taken the rupee down far enough when it hit 37.50, but by the end of the week the slide had resumed. \par \par \uc2  The RBI, with close to $30 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, has the ammunition to dominate the shallow market for a rupee which is only convertible on the current account. \par \par \uc2  Whatever the rupee's short term outlook, the very short term looks as precarious as Prime Minister Inder Kumar Gujral's minority government. \par \par \uc2  Gujral said on Saturday an election was "just around the corner." \par \par \uc2  His United Front (UF) alliance was set on Sunday to formally reject a Congress party demand for the UF to remove a coalition partner from government or lose Congress support. \par \par \uc2  One side may yet pull back from the brink, as a common perception is that the Hindu nationalist opposition Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP) has most to gain from a mid-term poll. \par \par \uc2  The constant concern that the minority government lacks power to follow through on reforms, and fear that it could fall whenever Congress chooses, along with worries about the rupee's future have all contributed to a slowdown in foreign direct investment. \par \par \uc2  FDI, in the first half of 1996/97 (April-March) was only around $1.5 billion, well under the $2.7 billion registered in the corresponding period last year, and monthly inflows have dwindled to just $100 million, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  An early decision on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least will reassure foreign investors, and will also help exports give impetus to an economy lacking domestic demand to fire up sluggish industrial growth, Gupta said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbn024hr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
