\par\par \b \uc2  India Rupee's Fall Seen Arrested For Now, Traders Say \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pankaj Aher \par \uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1997\par 12:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (AP-Dow Jones)--A strong statement from the Reserve Bank of India Tuesday seems to have arrested the rupee's recent plummet, but \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are divided about how long it will be before the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts testing new support levels. \par \par \uc2  Although the Reserve Bank has indicated it believes India's economic fundamentals, in particular its narrow current-account deficit, are sound, there is still a net demand for dollars, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has slipped steadily since the end of October on a combination of foreign investors selling shares to repatriate funds and importers buying dollars. But Tuesday it went into - by local standards - a freefall. It opened at 36.925 rupees to the dollar and had tumbled 1.6% to an intraday low of 37.52 by midday. \par \par \uc2  In contrast to recent sessions, in which the RBI is estimated to have spent several hundred million dollars in a largely unsuccessful effort to halt the rupee's descent, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted Tuesday to communicate its views via words not deeds. \par \par \uc2  'We believe that the adjustment that has already taken place in the value of the rupee is adequate,' Reserve Bank Governor C. Rangarajan said in a brief press statement. \par \par \uc2  'In view of the fundamentals, there is no ground for a further weakening of the rupee. Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any overshooting,' he added. \par \par \uc2  Within minutes, the rupee had jumped back to 37.22 to the dollar. It retreated lightly in the afternoon and ended at 37.39-.41, still well down from a Monday close of 36.88-.89. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the statement was more concretely worded than other comments from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few months, in particular the remark that there's no need for further rupee weakening. \par \par \uc2  'They have defined the cap at 37.50 rupees,' said Sunil Sharma, chief dealer at ANZ Grindlays Bank. 'But that statement and (recent) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to be further intensified if (the dollar) has to go below 37.20' rupees. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers said the current levels are likely to hold for a while. \par \par \uc2  'I think we will now see some consolidation for two weeks,' says K. Jayaram, senior dealer at Credit Lyonnais. 'We might see a revisit to 37.50 rupees, but that will be protected.' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 18-11-97 \par \par \uc2  1159GMT \par \par \uc2  1138 62549 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtbi08su9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
