\par\par \b \uc2  India: Bombay Shrs Down At Midday; IPCL Dn 6.0% \b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1997\par 08:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones Asian Equities Report \par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (Dow Jones)--Shares recovered slightly from early lows on the Bombay Stock Exchange around midday Tuesday due to a statement by the governor of the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a further depreciation of the rupee, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said remarks Tuesday by the Indian Prime Minister I.K. Gujral that 7% growth in Gross Domestic Product is still feasible have also helped trading sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The BSE Sensitive Index (Sensex) of 30 companies, which stood at 3544.27 in early trade, plunged to a low of 3494.80 points in late morning trade but recovered slightly when the rupee recovered from early lows. The index is at 3494.80 points, down 83.30 points, or 2.3%, from Monday's close of 3578.10 points. \par \par \uc2  On the National Stock Exchange (NSE), the NSE-50 index is at 1006 points, down 19.00 points, or 1.9%, from a previous close of 1025 points. \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds sold shares in a panic in early morning trading because of concern over the weakening of the Indian rupee. \par \par \uc2  The dollar touched a new 21-month high of 37.50 rupees but fell later to 37.35 rupees on the RBI governor's statements that the adjustment in the value of rupee is 'adequate' and there is no ground for further weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dealer at Pranav Securities Ltd. said the stock market moved up slightly on the RBI statement as well as the comments by Gujral at a meeting in New Delhi. However, the market is still bearish on increasing foreign fund selling because of volatility in other Asian regional stock exchanges. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at Mafatlal Securities Ltd. said most of the blue chip stocks are trading lower on selling pressure from foreign institutional investors and local traders. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Hindustan Lever Ltd., tobacco company, ITC Ltd. and Bharat Heavy Electricals Ltd. (BHEL) declined sharply on foreign fund selling. \par \par \uc2  Shares of Hindustan Lever are trading at 1269.00 rupees on the BSE at 0710 GMT, down 31.75 rupees, or 2.4%, from their previous close. \par \par \uc2  Shares of ITC are trading at 525.00 rupees, down 16.50 rupees, or 3.0%, from their last close. \par \par \uc2  Shares of BHEL are trading at 321.25 rupees, down 18.75 rupees, or 5.5%, from their previous close. \par \par \uc2  Shares of IPCL are trading lower on expectations of lower profits during the first-half of this financial year. IPCL stocks are trading at 79.00 rupees, down 5.00 rupees, or 6.0%, from their last close. \par \par \uc2  -By P. Ajaya Kumar; 91-22-288 4211; akkumar@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES INDIA REPORT  18-11-97 \par \par \uc2  0748GMT \par \par \uc2  5064 55106 \par \par \uc2 Dokument aer0000020011005dtbi004hg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
