\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee succumbs,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  draws line. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Cameron-Moore \par \uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1997\par 14:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 18 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee buckled on Tuesday, sinking to a 21-month low, and looked to be on a one-way street until the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the fall. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended the day at 37.40/42 to the dollar, sinking 1.4 percent from its close at 36.87/89 on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "It has been a hell of a move... And the rupee is not out of the woods yet," the head of treasury for a major international bank in Bombay said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee hit a low of 37.52, after which heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a warning from RBI governor Chakravarty Rangarajan that the selling should stop prompted a half-hearted rally. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that the adjustment that has already taken place in the value of the rupee is adequate," Rangarajan said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "In view of the fundamentals, there is no ground for a further weakening of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any overshooting." \par \par \uc2  Dealers say the RBI has spent close to $1 billion trying to slow the rupee's slide since the beginning of last week. \par \par \uc2  At its low, the rupee was five percent below early August levels of 35.70, which marked the start of pressure on the rupee after a year of stability. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action restored calm to a feverish market, but failed to wipe out bearishness toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It could go down a few percent more,"said Sandeep Nanda, head of research at NatWest Markets. NatWest's forecast of a rupee at 38.30 by March is unchanged but under review, he said. \par \par \uc2  Eyes will be on Bimal Jalan, who takes over from Rangarajan on Saturday as RBI governor, to see what stance he has toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI has espoused a policy of allowing the market to set the rupee's trend, while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth any volatility. \par \par \uc2  "The outlook depends a lot on investor confidence and there are some signs of portfolio inflows dwindling, in which case the rupee could fall a lot further," said Mark McFarland, regional economist for Peregrine Securities International Ltd in Hong Kong. "At the moment there is a loss of confidence," he said. \par \par \uc2  The international bank treasury head said uncertainties were hanging over the rupee during the next two months. \par \par \uc2  The political fallout from a report into the 1991 assassination of former prime minister Rajiv Gandhi is threatening to destabilise the minority government. \par \par \uc2  The report, due to be released in parliament later this week, could lay some blame at the feet of United Front coalition members, endangering Congress party support for the government. \par \par \uc2  And the ongoing turmoil in other Asian markets will continue to rub off on India. \par \par \uc2  Collapsing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere in Asia in the last few weeks heightened concern that the rupee was too strong, and when the Hong Kong market led a worldwide dive in stock markets three weeks ago, the rupee began a steady decline. \par \par \uc2  Rangarajan, whose five-year term is ending, said the rupee has been adjusting to the changing circumstances, but after Tuesday morning's precipitous move the necessary adjustment was complete. \par \par \uc2  He said India's economic fundamentals remained strong. \par \par \uc2  India's current account deficit as a proportion of gross domestic product is likely to be 1.4 percent in 1997/98 (April-March), up from 1.0 percent last year -- both very low levels for a developing country. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's strength was built on two pillars: high foreign capital inflows -- as investors streamed in after the economy began liberalising six years ago -- and low import demand. \par \par \uc2  The turn in sentiment against emerging markets after Asian markets imploded meant inflows would slow, economists said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign institutional investors have begun withdrawing funds from India, and an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a deterrent to money coming in, given the cheaper valuations available elsewhere, economists said. \par \par \uc2  There are also growing expectations that India's economic growth will soon start rising, prompting growing demand for imports and for dollars to pay for them. \par \par \uc2  REUTERS \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbi02dfe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
