\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee dips 1.4 pct, ends at 37.40/42 a dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 18 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee closed at 37.40/42 against the dollar ON Tuesday, registering a 1.4 percent compared with Monday's close after a day of volatile trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened at 36.90/93 compared with the previous close of 36.87/89 and started weakening fast. \par \par \uc2  Bank dealers and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokers attributed the rupee's fall to a rush for dollars by Indian corporates, but corporate treasurers and stock market analysts said inter-bank speculation played a complementary role. \par \par \uc2  "The market overreacted," said N.S.Paramasivam, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Essar Group, a leading Indian private business. \par \par \uc2  "It was a combination of inter-bank (covering) and moderate dollar demand (from corporates)," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the rupee's sharp movement on Tuesday was more panic-driven than volume-driven. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the fact that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the rupee breached the 37.50 level at noon signalled that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was happy with its fall. \par \par \uc2  The RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in small lots and immediately followed it up with a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which cooled the markets. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, RBI governor Chakravarty Rangarajan said he believed the rupee's adjustment was adequate. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that the adjustment that has already taken place in the value of the rupee is adequate," Rangarajan said. \par \par \uc2  "In view of the fundamentals, there is no ground for a further weakening of the rupee," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any overshooting," the statement warned. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the State Bank of India (SBI), a major dollar buyer in recent days, who bought on Tuesday morning, revisited the market in late afternoon, buying dollars at 36.40-plus levels. \par \par \uc2  They said the Indian Oil Corporation (IOC), India's largest refiner and monopoly importer of crude oil, has started buying dollars from the domestic market through IOC. \par \par \uc2  IOC has for over a year been raising dollars through offshore debt issues. \par \par \uc2  "It could be due to volatility in the foreign markets that IOC is buying dollars from the SBI," an official of the petroleum ministry told Reuters in New Delhi on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  There have been reports that IOC has exhausted its ECB (external commercial borrowing) limit of over $3.0 billion but the ministry official said he could not confirm this. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers expect the market to test the 37.50 level on Wednesday, but were divided on whether RBI will defend the rupee with all the resources at its command. \par \par \uc2  RBI's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves exceed $26.0 billion. \par \par \uc2  "RBI seems determined to keep the rupee at this level," said Rohit Malhotra, dealer at ABN-AMRO Bank N.V. in Bombay. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers were not sure. \par \par \uc2  "RBI does not seem to have sold much today," said a dealer at a leading foreign bank, referring to comments from some dealers that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold over $200 million on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "But market participants will be nervous at 37.50 levels," he said. "We will see this level tomorrow itself." \par \par \uc2  Frederick Castellino, chief dealer at brokers Mecklai & Mecklai said importers did not seem to be reassured by RBI's statement. \par \par \uc2  "They think further depreciation is in store. The market is still bearish on the rupee," he said. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a European bank said he saw the rupee dipping to 38 to the dollar by this month-end. \par \par \uc2  Forward premiums fell in tandem with recovery of the spot rupee but today's action was focused in the spot market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  R. Balakrishnan, president of Dil Vikas Finance Ltd, said foreign institutional investors (FIIs)'s selling of shares in the local market had little to do with the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Their (FIIs) net sales are less than $60 million -- it could not have caused today's sharp movement," he said. \par \par \uc2  "There has been no unusually large selling by FIIs in recent days," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbi02ddi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
