\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee buckles,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  says low enough. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Simon Cameron-Moore \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1997\par 10:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 18 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee buckled in early trade on Tuesday, sinking to a 21-month low, and looked to be on a one-way street until the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to give some support. \par \par \uc2  The rupee slumped in brisk trading to 37.52 to the dollar after opening at 36.90/93 and closing on Monday at 36.87/89. \par \par \uc2  But the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied immediately on RBI governor Chakravarty Rangarajan's warning to stop selling rupees and heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  By two p.m. (0830 GMT) the rupee stood at 37.35/38. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that the adjustment that has already taken place in the value of the rupee is adequate," Rangarajan said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "In view of the fundamentals, there is no ground for a further weakening of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any overshooting." \par \par \uc2  Dealers say the RBI has spent close to $1 billion trying to slow the rupee's slide since the beginning of last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around midday, just before Rangarajan's statement, as the rupee's decline gathered momentum. \par \par \uc2  "They really hammered the market at 37.50 so I think this should hold for some time. We are looking at 37.50 holding until March end," said Shekhar Sathe, senior vice-president at Kotak Mahindra Capital Company. \par \par \uc2  Earlier, Indian corporates rushed to buy dollars and banks began speculating against the rupee while the RBI held to the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  "Corporates are panicking and with RBI keeping away, it has become a one-way market," said a dealer at a private bank at the height of the action. \par \par \uc2  At its low, the rupee was five percent below early Agust levels of 35.70, which marked the start of pressure on the rupee after a year of stability. \par \par \uc2  "The market is slightly comforted now after the governor's statement that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any overshooting of the rupee," said N.S. Paramasivam, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the private Essar Group. \par \par \uc2  Collapsing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere in Asia heightened concern that the rupee was too strong, and when the Hong Kong market led a worldwide dive in stock markets three weeks ago the rupee began a steady decline. \par \par \uc2  But Rangarajan, who will pass the governership to Bimal Jalan on Saturday after a five year term, said the rupee remained stable through the turbulence in East Asian markets and its fundamentals, particularly in the external sector, remained strong. \par \par \uc2  He said the rupee has been adjusting to the changing circumstances, but after Tuesday morning's precipitous move the necessary adjustment was complete. \par \par \uc2  India's current account deficit as a proportion of gross domestic product is likely to be 1.4 percent in 1997/98 (April-March), up from 1.0 percent last year -- both very low levels for a developing country. \par \par \uc2  The rupee's strength was built on two pillars - high foreign capital inflows -- as investors seized opportunities offered by an economy which began liberalising six years ago -- and low import demand. \par \par \uc2  The turn in sentiment against emerging markets following a bloodletting in Asian markets meant inflows would slow, economists said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign institutional investors have begun withdrawing funds from India, and an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be a deterrent to money coming in, given the cheaper valuations available elsewhere, economists said. \par \par \uc2  There are also growing expectations that India's economic growth will soon start rising, prompting growing demand for imports and for dollars to pay for them. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbi02dcb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
