\par\par \b \uc2  Indian  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market calm after RBI chief's remarks. \b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 18 (Reuters)  - Calm returned to the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the rupee traded quietly on Tuesday afternoon after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said he considered the latest "adjustment" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value was "adequate." \par \par \uc2  The spot rupee recovered to 37.25/33 from 37.40 levels after the governor's statement, dealers said. It was quoted at a wide 37.29/31 at 1.0 p.m. \par \par \uc2  The rupee had hit a 21-month low of 37.52 earlier on Tuesday after slumping from an open at 36.90/93. \par \par \uc2  Forward dollar premiums fell by around 10 paise as the rupee recovered, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "While the RBI has been ensuring that there is no undue volatility and that there are orderly conditions in the market, the rupee has been adjusting to the changing circumstances," the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) governor Chakravarty Rangarajan said in his statement. \par \par \uc2  "We believe that the adjustment that has already taken place in the value of the rupee is adequate," he said. \par \par \uc2  "In view of the fundamentals, there is no ground for a further weakening of the rupee: Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not hesitate to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any overshooting," he added. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbi02dc0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex1