\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee sinks as firms rush for dollar cover. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1997\par 16:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee sank to a new 21-month low on Monday as firms rushed for cover amid signs of a slowdown in foreign capital inflows and indications that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a big way, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee ended Monday at 36.875/895 compared to a previous close of 36.75/76 on Thursday -- most banks were shut for a religious holiday on Friday. Dealers expected the rupee to breach the 36.90 level on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Indian firms are covering their unhedged exposures," said a dealer at a leading Bombay bank. "The realisation has dawned on them that the dollar has only one way to go," he said. \par \par \uc2  The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an American bank agreed. \par \par \uc2  "There is widespread corporate buying today," he said. "And dollar supply appears to be drying up." \par \par \uc2  India's capital market regulator, the Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI), said on Monday net portfolio holdings by foreign institutional investors (FIIs) fell by $58.4 million between November 1 and 14. \par \par \uc2  SEBI said cumulative FII investments dropped to $9.05 billion from $9.10 billion on November 10. \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds have been under pressure to send money home after taking losses on other Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  But some brokers said there was a growing perception among fund managers that India had no alternative but to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate following the collapse in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values. \par \par \uc2  The rupee is now just over three percent below its early August levels, when a comment by RBI deputy governor Y.V. Reddy that it appeared to be overvalued in trade-weighted terms triggered pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI)'s \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy has also unnerved Indian firms which believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would mobilise its $26.27 billion foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to draw a line under the rupee's fall. \par \par \uc2  "Last week they vigorously defended at 36.75 level, today they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a small way but allowed it to slip further," said a corporate treasury executive. \par \par \uc2  "It is clear they only want to smooth the (rupee's) fall, not arrest it," he said. Indian exporters have been clamouring for a weaker rupee, saying their exports have become uncompetitive after a sharp devaluation by their Asian neighbours. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers said political worries had little to do with the rupee's weakening. Historically, the Indian rupee has been immune to political crises. \par \par \uc2  India is bracing for the presentation of a report on an investigation into the 1991 assassination of former prime minister Rajiv Gandhi, which could undermine the United Front coalition government's stability. \par \par \uc2  The minority government led by Prime Minister Inder Kumar Gujral depends on Congress party support for survival, and Congress has already threatened to reconsider support for the government over the report into the killing of its former leader. \par \par \uc2  Arbitraging between the local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the overseas non-deliverable forward (NDF) market has been keeping the local forward dollar premiums on the higher side, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds are not allowed to hedge their equity investments in India and can hedge only in the NDF markets. \par \par \uc2  Some Indian and foreign entities are selling forward dollars in the NDF market, taking advantage of higher overseas premium and buying cheaper forward dollars at home, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Indian firms which borrowed cheap offshore loans are also seeking cover. \par \par \uc2  Many Indian firms borrowed overseas when the rupee was steady and the local six month annualised forward dollar premium was at six percent or lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "With six month premium now ruling at over seven percent and spot rupee weakening, most of these firms are scared and rushing for cover," said a company executive. \par \par \uc2  The fact that forward dollar premiums have not traced a fall in domestic interest rates reflects the demand-supply mismatch, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbh02jpj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
