\par\par \b \uc2  India: Rupee Recovers From New 20-Mo Low But Closes Down \b0\par\par\uc2 319 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 1997\par 12:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee closed lower Thursday, but off a new 20-month low, because the market stabilized after the governor of the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the 'rupee doesn't reflect fundamentals,' traders said. \par \par \uc2  At the 1030 GMT (5:30 am EST) close of trading Wednesday the dollar was between 36.74-.76 rupees, up from between 36.69-.70 rupees at the close of trading Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar opened Thursday between 36.74-.76 rupees then rose immediately to trade at a new 20-month high of 36.755 rupees within the first 10 minutes of trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar then slipped below 36.70 rupees briefly in morning trading after the RBI Governor C. Rangarajan made his comments at a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conference in Colombo. However, the dollar recovered on continued demand from companies. \par \par \uc2  Rangarajan said the country's current account deficit supports a stable rupee. \par \par \uc2  India's current account deficit is around 1% of Gross Domestic Product and the total debt to GDP ratio was 26% as of March 1997. India's short-term debt amounts to about 7.4% of total debt. \par \par \uc2  Continued support at lower levels kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 36.70 rupees Thursday. \par \par \uc2  In the forward market, the premiums rose in early trading but fell back after the spot dollar stabilized, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The premium for May-dollars closed between 1.50 rupees and 1.55 rupees. It had closed Wednesday between 1.40 rupees and 1.42 rupees. The premium for October dollars closed between 2.54 rupees and 2.59 rupees. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of India is understood to have sold $200 million Tuesday and another $360 million Wednesday to stem the slide of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-11-97 \par \par \uc2  1102GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtbd08o1w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
