\par\par \b \uc2  Indian  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  races to support falling rupee. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in its own words, "aggressively and repeatedly" on Wednesday to support a fast-weakening rupee. \par \par \uc2  The rupee at one stage touched 36.75 to the dollar -- its weakest level since February 1996. \par \par \uc2  But persistent Reserve Bank of India (RBI) dollar selling, countering mounting demand from importers and firms needing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to repay offshore debt, helped the rupee recover slightly by the close . \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively and repeatedly today," an RBI spokeswoman told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold more than $400 million on Wednesday, over double the amount it threw at the market on Tuesday to cushion the rupee's slide. \par \par \uc2  "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be some sort of record," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has depreciated by nearly 1.5 percent since markets in Asia began plunging three weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  While the scale of the decline pales beside other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indian importers, anticipating further falls, scrambled for dollar cover as the weakness became manifest. \par \par \uc2  "The RBI is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid (\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  becoming) another victim of the Asian markets contagion," said a senior dealer at Societe Generale bank in Bombay. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the day at 36.69/71 to the dollar, compared with 36.63/65 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began its defence soon after trade started as the rupee crossed the 36.70 level. \par \par \uc2  "They (the RBI) seem to be comfortable with 36.70 for today," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI was a net buyer of dollars until August, as it tried to moderate the rupee's strength against a dollar which ruled international exchanges. \par \par \uc2  But it turned dollar seller in September after the market took fright at comments from a senior RBI official in August that the rupee appeared overvalued on a trade weighted basis. \par \par \uc2  The rupee reverted back to slow motion until the last couple of weeks, when treasury managers said a series of developments put the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the firing line. \par \par \uc2  Worries about a slowdown in capital inflows into India followed in the wake of the Asian markets crisis. \par \par \uc2  An $800 million global depositary receipts (GDR) issue of the state-run Gas Authority of India Ltd (GAIL) was deferred earlier this month as investors backed away from emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  Newspapers on Wednesday said a planned GDR issue for another state-run firm, Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Ltd (MTNL) could suffer the same fate, although the government said no decision had been taken on whether to postpone roadshows for the launch. \par \par \uc2  Investor disenchantment with emerging markets also sent the Bombay stock market reeling on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Bombay Stock Exchange top 30 index fell 2.42 percent to a seven month low of 3,633.18, down 90.22 points, as foreign funds accelerated selling to meet redemption pressures. \par \par \uc2  A statement by the RBI governor Chakravarty Rangarajan last week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if there was a "high degree" of volatility in the exchange market had been interpreted by dealers as a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not mind a weaker rupee. \par \par \uc2  Senior commerce ministry officials fuelled the speculation by saying the fall in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a worry for Indian exporters. \par \par \uc2  Despite the latest fall, the rupee remained overvalued, Indian exporters and some economists said. "We had expected much of this (weakening) to happen in the last quarter of 1997/98 (April-March)," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbc02kjo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
