\par\par \b \uc2  Indian rupee ends weak despite large RBI dlr sales. \b0\par\par\uc2 555 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended at 36.69/71 against the dollar on a volatile Wednesday marked by aggressive and persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to arrest the fall in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened the day at 36.65/67 to a dollar compared to the previous close of 36.63/65 and quickly began losing ground. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI), which has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Monday, stepped in only after the rupee breached the key 36.70-barrier, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Thereafter, the RBI hit the market repeatedly, selling dollars every time the rupee flared up, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "They began selling at 36.70/71 and continued upto 36.74," said the chief dealer at a Bombay bank. \par \par \uc2  After the market closed, an RBI spokeswoman confirmed the aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively and repeatedly today," she told Reuters, but declined to quantify. \par \par \uc2  Dealers conservatively put today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice Tuesday's estimated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of $200 million. \par \par \uc2  "They must have sold $150 to $200 in the spot and over $200 in the swaps market today," said the chief dealer at a bank. \par \par \uc2  The spot rupee saw a low of 36.75 after the State Bank of India (SBI) entered the market as a dollar buyer. \par \par \uc2  After SBI left the market, the rupee rose briefly to 36.66 before weakening again to 36.72, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  RBI was more aggressive in the forward dollar segment, pulling down premiums by as much as 20 to 25 paise for far-forward maturity dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  October 1998 dollars closed at 2.32/2.35 rupees compared to Tuesday's close of 2.57/2.62 rupees. \par \par \uc2  "Their main aim appears to be to bring down forwards," said Sharukh Wadia, chief dealer at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  "Premiums are expected to come down," he said. \par \par \uc2  The six month annualised premium closed Wednesday at 7.15 percent compared with the previous 7.47 percent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A dealer said dollar sales from Indian exporters have shrunk after some of them started selling dollars in the overseas non-deliverable forward (NDF) markets. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors are not allowed to hedge exchange risk for their Indian investments, especially in the stock market. The overseas NDF market allows them to hedge \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks. \par \par \uc2  The rupee had weakened in the NDF market following Asian stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis but the gap between premiums in the NDF market and the Indian market has narrowed since last week. \par \par \uc2  Most dealers are looking forward to further, though staggered, depreciation of the rupee. \par \par \uc2  "There is enough corporate demand (for dollars)," said Sunil Sharma, chief dealer at ANZ Grindlays Bank. He said RBI will have to continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the pace. \par \par \uc2  Standard Chartered's Wadia said he expected RBI to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again as the spot rupee hit the 36.75 level. \par \par \uc2  "RBI will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent sharp falls," said a dealer at a public sector bank. He said the rupee was still overvalued and needed further correction to reflect the sharp devaluations in neighbouring countries that hurt Indian competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  "But they are happy with the 36.70 level for now," he said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbc02kho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
