\par\par \b \uc2  Indian gov't will not interfere in rupee--Ramaiah. \b0\par\par\uc2 274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW DELHI, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The Indian government will not interfere in the value of the rupee, Commerce Minister Bolla Buli Ramaiah told Reuters on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The government will not interfere in the rupee," Ramaiah said. "We do not anticipate a crisis in exports. It is doing well." \par \par \uc2  Ramaiah did not elaborate, but noted that India's exports had risen 11.6 percent in September over a year earlier to $2.9 billion. \par \par \uc2  He said a high-level meeting to be chaired by Prime Minister Inder Kumar Gujral on the competitiveness of India's exports, which had been originally scheduled for Wednesday, had been postponed to November 17. \par \par \uc2  Ramaiah was speaking on the fringes of a businessmen's meeting he spoke at. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Monday to sell dollars to cushion the rupee. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market on Wednesday after the rupee breached the key 36.70 per dollar level. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed on Tuesday at 36.635/650 to the dollar. It had opened at 36.38/40 to the dollar on November 6, before RBI Governor Chakravarty Rangarajan said the centralbank would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if there was a "high degree" of volatility. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have said the rising dollar demand stemmed from importers, and firms seeking to hedge their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowings in the face of a falling rupee as well as those seeking to repay offshore loans. \par \par \uc2  -- Giriprakash Krishnamoorthy, New Delhi Newsroom +91-11-301-2024; Fax +91-11-301-4043 \par \par \uc2  -- E-mail: delhi.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbc02kfs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
