\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-India rupee seen under pressure vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Giri Venkatesan \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 10 (Reuters) - Dollar demand from importers will keep the Indian rupee under pressure this week but sharp falls in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value are unlikely, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We expect the rupee to touch 36.60 (per dollar) before the end of the week," said a dealer at a private bank. \par \par \uc2  "Corporate demand (for dollars) remains fairly strong but we do not see the rupee weakening below that," he said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has been dropping at a rate of about 10 paise (0.1 ruppee) a week since the recent convulsions in East Asian financial markets began just over two weeks ago, but it is still only about one percent lower against the dollar, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have plunged. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed Friday at 36.53/54 against the previous week's close of 36.37/38, a fall of 0.4 percent after the government postponed a mega-issue of global depositary receipts (GDRs). \par \par \uc2  The government last week decided to defer a GDR issue by the state-run Gas Authority of India Ltd (GAIL) after it ran into pricing problems. The issue was expected to raise around $800 million. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, the rupee was due for a correction," said a dealer at a European bank. "It is certainly overvalued." \par \par \uc2  The dealer said he expected the rupee to weaken to 37 per dollar by the end of 1997. \par \par \uc2  "I think at some time or the other the turmoil in other Asian markets was bound to affect us," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee had remained largely unshaken through the current crisis in Asian markets, which began last summer with the devaluations of several regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The decision to defer the GAIL issue triggered the fall in the rupee but there were a number of events which contributed," he said. \par \par \uc2  Besides the GAIL GDR postponement, a statement by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) governor that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market only to check high volatility also contributed to the rupee's weakening, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if there is a high degree of volatility," RBI governor Chakravarty Rangarajan told reporters on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Market players have decided the RBI will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the rupee weakens very sharply," said a dealer at a state-run bank. \par \par \uc2  "We will probably see the rupee close weaker by say 3-5 paise (0.03-0.05 rupee) every day for the next few days," he said. "We expect the RBI to start defending the rupee as it weakens past 36.70 levels." \par \par \uc2  Dealers said demand from importers was strong but there were no indications of panic covering which could spark a free fall. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at the movements in the (exchange) rate, volatility has not really been much," said a dealer at a private bank. "A drop of a few paise is nothing great." \par \par \uc2  "There are signs the market is maturing," he added. \par \par \uc2  On Friday the rupee fell nine paise, but later recovered as exporters nipped in to sell dollars. \par \par \uc2  "In earlier days, there used to be one-way movement which would be reversed only by RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This time we saw exporters were quite willing to sell at higher levels," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers, however, expect the rupee to strengthen against the forward dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think current levels of forward premiums do not reflect interest rates differentials (between India and the U.S)," said a dealer. "They should be much lower...say around 4.5 percent." \par \par \uc2  The annualised premium on dollars for six-month forward delivery closed on Friday at 7.27 percent against 6.36 percent the previous week. \par \par \uc2  "But premiums may not fall at once," said the dealer. "There will continue to be some covering (by importers) although current levels favour exporters more than importers." \par \par \uc2  The dealer said he expects the RBI to sell forward dollars if the rise in premiums is not checked. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtba02x39\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
