\par\par \b \uc2  India sells dollars to brake rupee's fall. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shyam Kumar \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets twice on Monday to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide which dealers said would continue to be fuelled by corporate demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  The rupee closed on Monday at 36.58/59 to the dollar, forced unrelentingly down by purchases of dollars from a handful of the country's banks, chief among them the largest commercial bank, the State Bank of India (SBI). \par \par \uc2  "The rupee fell to 36.58/59 at close mainly on SBI's demand," said a dealer at a public sector bank. "This indicates rising corporate demand (for dollars) which will break the 36.60-mark." \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Monday, two bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) had helped stop the rupee from hitting the 36.60 level, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened steady at 36.52/54 a dollar and began retreating immediately, they said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) started selling at 36.58 levels, after the rupee slipped to 36.59 a dollar. \par \par \uc2  "They began selling at 36.58 and later at lower levels," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said they had expected RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the rupee approached the 36.60-mark, but others said they were surprised since there had been no sharp swings in the exchange rate on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The rupee weakened much more on Friday, but they were not around," said a dealer at a European bank about RBI. \par \par \uc2  After RBI's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupee stabilised at 36.55/56 until about 3.30 p.m. (1000 GMT), when dollar demand from the State Bank of India (SBI) and a handful of other banks dragged the rupee to its earlier lows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee, trading in a 36.30/40 band in recent weeks, breached the 36.50-level on Friday on the back of an RBI statement on its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  RBI governor Chakravarty Rangarajan told reporters the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only in case of "a high degree of volatility". \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the use of that phrase was interpreted by the market to mean that the RBI was willing to see faster depreciation of the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Rangarajan's remark followed closely after the government deferred an $800-million issue of global depositary receipts (GDR) by the state-run Gas Authority of India Ltd (GAIL) because of poor investor response. \par \par \uc2  The deferral of the issue along with other offshore debt issues by Indian firms following the turbulence in Asian markets prompted importers and borrowers to rush for cover on fears of a slowdown in foreign capital inflows, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said several other proposed overseas equity issues could be adversely affected by a weak global capital market. \par \par \uc2  "A weak global stock market is likely to have adverse effect on the Indian stock market and may have wide ramifications on the future of the rupee," Societe Generale said in a report. \par \par \uc2  Recent trends suggesting a reversal of a slowdown in Indian industrial growth could spur import demand, adding to the pressure on the rupee, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee has been dropping at a rate of about 10 paise (0.10 rupee) a week since the recent convulsions in East Asian financial markets began just over two weeks ago, but it is still only about one percent lower against the dollar, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have plunged. \par \par \uc2  This has rendered many Indian exports uncompetitive and the RBI will have no alternative but to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken further, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtba02ouf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
