\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee holds at 36.55 vs dlr after sharp drop. \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 7 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee stabilised at 36.55/57 to a dollar on Friday morning as banks sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of exporters, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Some banks have sold (dollars)," said a dealer. \par \par \uc2  He said these sales helped the rupee recover to 36.55 after seeing a low of 36.58 earlier on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened Friday at 36.49/51 compared to the previous close of 36.47/48 and slipped fast on nervous trade, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a postponement of a $800 million overseas equity issue by the state-run Gas Authority of India Ltd (GAIL) on Wednesday affected the market's sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) governor's remarks on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy on Thursday have been interpreted by the market to mean willing to see the rupee weaken further, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The RBI governor said on Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  only if there is a high degree of volatility. \par \par \uc2  "The market seems to have interpreted this to mean the RBI is open to further weakening of the rupee," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay Newsroom (91) 22-265 9000; Fax (91) 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email: bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb702uc0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
