\par\par \b \uc2  India: Rupee Closes Stronger On Early Dollar Selling \b0\par\par\uc2 260 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 1997\par 13:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee closed stronger Monday because of some early dollar selling by banks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At the 1030 GMT (6:30 a.m. EDT) close of trading Monday the dollar was between 36.2125 rupees and 36.2175 rupees, down from Friday's close of between 36.2250 rupees and 36.2350 rupees. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said banks sold dollars early in the day on expectations of increased dollar inflows accumulated over the long weekend. \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar selling didn't continue due to fears that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars at below the 36.20 rupee mark, traders said. \par \par \uc2  'Other than the morning sell off' there wasn't much of trading in the market, said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  asked for dollar quotes from banks earlier in the day when the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded briefly at around 36.2050 rupees but it didn't buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  The dollar didn't fall below the 36.20 rupees mark because State Bank of India, the country's largest commercial bank, bought dollars. \par \par \uc2  '(State Bank) bought spot dollar until around 36.2150 rupees and made forward dollar purchases (for delivery at end October and November),' said a dealer at a state-owned bank. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a foreign bank said State Bank bid for the dollar at 36.2150 rupees until the close of trading Monday. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-10-97 \par \par \uc2  1128GMT \par \par \uc2  1164 1847 62506 \par \