\par\par \b \uc2  India rupee ends steady, RBI in passive dlr buying. \b0\par\par\uc2 227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Oct 1 (Reuter) - The Indian rupee closed steady at 36.18/19 against the dollar on Wednesday amid feeble signs of emerging corporate demand for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBI) was passively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on Wednesday, buying dollars from public sector banks which approached the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell dollars, they said. \par \par \uc2  Some foreign banks also said they sold dollars to the RBI. \par \par \uc2  The rupee opened at 36.175/190 to a dollar. The morning session saw good inflows, including foreign direct investment from a telecommunications project, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The rupee saw a high of 36.150 and a high of 36.1850 on Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The afternoon session saw dollar buying by the State Bank of India (SBI), Bombay dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A European bank was also buying dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "We have started seeing some dollar demand today," said a dealer. He said corporates were covering short-term dollar requirements. \par \par \uc2  Forward premiums fell in the afternoon, tracking a fall in call money rates, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The six month annualised forward premium closed Wednesday at 5.33 percent compared to the previous close of 5.54 percent. \par \par \uc2  -- Bombay newsroom (91)22-265 9000, Fax 22-264 1699 \par \par \uc2  -- Email:bombay.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dta101m4f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\